A||||3||JohnnyK||Now one headlight doesn't raise or lower??||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||01-31-2001||03:45 PM||||Well, I connected those plugs for each headlight that I disconnected before the winter, and now the left one won't go up or down.  It's not the plug. There is no corrosion (I covered it up) and I've wiggled it and nothing. But it does click, but it could just be the actual lightbulb turning on? Do those click? The motor doesn't turn or anything like that, so it's not a matter of rebuild. Just seems there is no power going to that one? Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fformula88||01-31-2001||04:55 PM||||Maybe its getting caught on a wire down in there.  If its an 87/88, the headlight motor shuts itself off when there is increased resisitance on the headlight.  In other words, when it cannot move the motor shuts iuitself off.  This keeps them from stripping out if there is too much snow over them, or other hazards.<P>Other than that I would double check the connections... maybe spray some electrical cleaner in them to be sure they didn't get any corrosion in them.  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnnyK||01-31-2001||05:44 PM||||Nope, it moves freely. Does anyone know if a headlight is supposed to click (the bulb) or is that something trying to work?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||02-02-2001||10:34 AM||||I think the lights do make a fairly loud click when they come on up front.  So I'd say thats normal.  Do you have a testing light?  You might want try testing the wiring to see if there is power going to the motor.  ||||reg||1
